@*
    One sanctioned, inert suggestion for a detail the email did not state.

    The value is shown and explained but never written into the bound field until the traveler
    presses Use this. That is the whole point: a leg must not carry a value the traveler did not
    see and accept, so this renders a proposal rather than applying a default (FR-028, FR-034).

    Taking the proposal as a parameter rather than computing one internally means a second
    sanctioned default arrives as data, not as another component.
*@
<div class="border-start border-3 ps-2 mt-1 small text-body-secondary" role="note" data-testid="draft-suggestion-@Field">
    <span>@Why</span>
    <button type="button" class="btn btn-link btn-sm p-0 ms-1 align-baseline" data-testid="draft-suggestion-accept-@Field" @onclick="AcceptAsync">
        Use this
    </button>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>Field name this suggestion belongs to, used only to key the test hooks.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Field { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>The sentence explaining what was missing and what is being proposed.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Why { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Raised only when the traveler presses Use this.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback OnAccept { get; set; }

    private Task AcceptAsync() => OnAccept.InvokeAsync();
}
